<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-1-5-programming-environments" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Programming Environments</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The best way to learn a programming language is to program with it! To do that,
      we’ll need to install a development environment. In this section, we’ll discuss
      installing the Java JDK, running programs from the command line, installing and
      using the Eclipse integrated development environment (IDE) to write, run, and debug
      Java programs, and using JShell to test small blocks of code.
    </p>

    <p>
      When you look for Java software online, you will frequently encounter three related
      acronyms: JVM, JRE, and JDK. Because they sound similar, beginners often wonder which
      one they need to install.
    </p>

    <p>
      To understand the difference, it helps to think of them as a set of nested boxes,
      where each larger box contains the smaller one plus additional tools:
    </p>

     <figure xml:id="figure-java-platform-hierarchy">
      <caption>The Java platform hierarchy</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_1_4.png" width="85%">
        <shortdescription>Concentric diagram of the Java platform: JDK containing JRE, which contains the JVM.</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A concentric nested box diagram illustrating the Java platform hierarchy. The
            outermost box represents the Java Development Kit (JDK), which contains
            development tools including javac, jshell, javadoc, and the debugger. Nested
            inside the JDK is the Java Runtime Environment (JRE), which contains the core
            Java class libraries such as String, Math, Scanner, System, and Collections.
            Nested inside the JRE is the innermost box, the Java Virtual Machine (JVM),
            which executes compiled Java bytecode on the host operating system.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Here is what each component does:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <line><strong>The JVM (Java Virtual Machine): The Execution Engine</strong></line>
          The JVM is the software program that executes Java bytecode instructions on your
          physical computer. Every operating system (Windows, macOS, Linux) has its own
          specific JVM built to translate bytecode into that computer's native machine
          language.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <line><strong>The JRE (Java Runtime Environment): For Running Programs</strong></line>
          The JRE contains the JVM plus the standard Java class libraries (all the
          pre-written Java code that provides basic features like math operations, text
          handling, and input reading).
        </p>
        <p>
          The JRE was historically designed for end users. If someone only wanted to run a
          game or a business application written in Java on their computer—without writing
          any code themselves—they only needed to install the JRE. The JRE can execute
          programs, but it cannot compile or build new ones.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <line><strong>The JDK (Java Development Kit): For Creating Programs</strong></line>
          The JDK is the complete software package designed for programmers. It contains
          everything in the JRE (so you can run programs), plus all the developer tools
          needed to create software from scratch.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most importantly, the JDK includes:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <c>javac</c> (The Java Compiler): The tool that translates your human-readable
              <c>.java</c> source code files into <c>.class</c> bytecode files.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <c>jshell</c>: The interactive command-line shell you will use in this
              chapter to test Java code snippets instantly.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <c>java</c>: The command that launches the JVM to run compiled applications.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Other utility tools, such as <c>jar</c> (for packaging applications) and
              <c>javadoc</c> (for generating documentation).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      If you read older Java textbooks or tutorials, you may see instructions telling you
      to <q>download the JRE if you are a user or download the JDK if you are a programmer.</q>
    </p>

    <p>
      In modern Java (versions 11 and newer), this distinction has been simplified:
      standalone JRE downloads have been retired.
    </p>

    <p>
      Today, anyone who needs to run or write Java simply downloads and installs the JDK
      (such as the standard OpenJDK distributions like Eclipse Temurin). When you install
      the JDK on Windows, macOS, or Linux, it automatically provides both the execution
      environment to run programs and the developer tools you need to write them.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-1-5-1-getting-jdk">
    <title>Getting a JDK</title>

    <p>
      Because the Java platform is maintained through the open-source OpenJDK project,
      there is no single <q>exclusive</q> JDK—instead, several trusted vendors package
      and distribute certified builds of the exact same underlying technology:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Eclipse Temurin (by Adoptium):</strong> A community-driven,
          vendor-neutral distribution managed by the Eclipse Foundation; widely popular in
          education and industry for its reliability and open-source licensing.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Oracle OpenJDK / Oracle JDK:</strong> The official builds provided
          directly by Oracle (the stewards of the Java language).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Cloud &amp; Enterprise Distributions:</strong> Other well-maintained,
          free builds backed by major tech companies, including Amazon Corretto, Microsoft
          Build of OpenJDK, and Azul Zulu.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      All certified distributions adhere strictly to the official Java SE (Standard
      Edition) specifications, meaning your code will compile and run identically
      regardless of which vendor's JDK you install. For this book, downloading a recent
      Long-Term Support (LTS) release (such as Java 21 or Java 25) from Eclipse Adoptium at
      <url href="https://adoptium.net/">https://adoptium.net/</url> is an ideal,
      hassle-free choice.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once you’ve downloaded the installer, run it to start the installation. On Windows,
      during the setup wizard, you will see a list of optional features. Make sure that
      both <strong>Add to PATH</strong> and <strong>Set JAVA_HOME variable</strong> are
      selected. This ensures you can run Java tools directly from the command line. On
      macOS and Linux, run the package installer and follow the standard on-screen prompts.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once installed, you will be able to run the <c>java</c>, <c>javac</c>, and
      <c>jshell</c> commands directly from your computer’s terminal or command prompt.
      (There is more detail about the command prompt in the next section of this chapter,
      but for a quick start, on Windows, press the Windows key and search for <c>cmd</c>;
      on macOS, press Command-Space and search for Terminal.) To confirm that Java is
      installed and properly configured, open a new terminal or command prompt window
      (opening a new window ensures your system recognizes the newly installed software) and
      type <c>jshell</c>. If your installation succeeded, you will see a brief welcome
      message followed by an interactive prompt, as in the session below. To exit JShell
      and return to your operating system prompt, simply type <c>/exit</c> and press Enter.
    </p>

    <console>
      <input>jshell</input>
      <output>
|  Welcome to JShell -- Version 25.0.4.1
|  For an introduction type: /help intro
      </output>
      <input>/exit</input>
      <output>
|  Goodbye
      </output>
    </console>

    <p>
      If your computer displays an error like <q>command not found,</q> try closing and
      reopening your command prompt or restarting your computer and reopening the command
      prompt. If the command still fails, re-run the installer and ensure the option to Add
      to PATH was selected. These will fix common installation errors, but if neither
      works, consult your instructor.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-1-5-2-command-line">
    <title>Java from the Command Line</title>

    <p>
      You will usually be writing, editing, running, and debugging your programs in an IDE.
      In this text, we will use the Eclipse IDE. But it is important that you can also run
      your Java programs from the command line. For example, if you are unable to run a
      program in your IDE, you can try running it at the command line to confirm that your
      Java installation is not the cause of the issue. There might also be times when you
      don’t want the overhead of using the IDE – for a quick check of some code, for
      example.
    </p>

    <p>
      You might find the command line environment unfamiliar and unintuitive at first.
      However, it takes only a little practice to learn the basics and to become productive
      using it. It is useful to know how to use the command line, and it is particularly
      important for computer science students.
    </p>

    <p>
      On Windows, you interact with the command line using the Command Prompt or
      PowerShell. On macOS and Linux, you use the application called Terminal. In the
      command line application, you will type a command at the prompt and press Enter. The
      computer will carry out the command, displaying any output in the command window, and
      will then redisplay the prompt so that you can type another command.
    </p>

    <p>
      A central concept of the command-line environment is the current directory (also
      called the working directory). A directory is simply the technical term for a folder.
      The working directory is the folder on your computer that the command prompt is
      currently focused on; any files you create, compile, or run will be looked for in
      this folder. Usually, the name or path of the current directory is displayed
      directly as part of the command prompt itself.
    </p>

    <p>
      When you first open a command prompt or terminal, your working directory defaults to
      your personal home directory (e.g., <c>C:\Users\YourName</c> on Windows, or
      <c>/Users/YourName</c> on macOS).
    </p>

    <p>
      To see the contents of your current directory on Windows Command Prompt, type
      <c>dir</c> and press Enter. On macOS, Linux, and Windows PowerShell, type <c>ls</c>
      and press Enter. To navigate into a different folder, use the <c>cd</c> (change
      directory) command followed by the name of the folder. To move into a subfolder,
      simply type the name of the folder. For example, if you are in your home directory
      and want to enter your Desktop folder, type <c>cd Desktop</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To move down multiple levels, provide the path. For example, if you want to move into
      a folder named Programs located inside Desktop from your home directory, type <c>cd</c>
      followed by the path, where each folder name is separated by a slash, e.g. <c>cd
      Desktop/Programs</c> (Note: macOS and Linux use forward slashes <c>/</c>, while
      Windows traditionally uses backslashes <c>\</c>, though modern Windows tools accept
      forward slashes too.)
    </p>

    <p>
      To return to the parent directory (one level up), type <c>cd</c> followed by a space
      and two dots, e.g. <c>cd ..</c> For example, if you are inside Programs, typing
      <c>cd ..</c> takes you back up to Desktop.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here are a couple of tips to prevent common errors. If a folder name contains
      spaces, enclose it in quotes, e.g. <c>cd "Java Projects"</c>. You rarely need to
      type full folder names by hand. Type the first few letters (such as <c>cd Desk</c>)
      and press the <kbd>Tab</kbd> key; your computer will automatically fill in the rest
      of the name. If multiple folders match, pressing <kbd>Tab</kbd> a second time will
      show you the options.
    </p>

    <p>
      To create a folder called JavaPrograms at the command line, first navigate to the
      parent folder (the folder that will hold your new JavaPrograms folder). Then use the
      <c>mkdir</c> command followed by the name of the new directory, e.g. <c>mkdir
      JavaPrograms</c> (followed by Enter, as always). Note that this will create the
      directory but will not change the current working directory. If you list the
      directory contents after this command, you should see the contents of the parent
      directory, with your new directory in its alphabetical position in the list. If you
      want to make the new directory your working directory, remember to <c>cd</c> into the
      new directory with <c>cd JavaPrograms</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let’s write, compile, and run a Java program now.
    </p>

    <p>
      You will need a text editor to write and edit Java programs that you are going to
      compile and run at the command line. A text editor is a program that allows you to
      write and save programs as plain text – text that is characters only, with no encoded
      formatting information. Text editors that are designed for programmers contain
      language-sensitive editing features such as syntax coloring and auto-complete that
      make it easier to write programs.
    </p>

    <p>
      You are free to use any plain-text editor for your operating system, but the options
      suggested here provide line numbers and Java syntax highlighting. On Linux, you can
      use Gedit (often pre-installed as Text Editor). On Windows, Notepad++ is a popular
      free download, while Mac users often prefer BBEdit, which provides a robust free
      version for code editing. If you want a versatile, modern editor that works
      identically on Windows, macOS, and Linux, Microsoft's Visual Studio Code (VS Code) is
      another excellent free option.
    </p>

    <p>
      The process for writing a program is first typing the program into the text editor,
      then saving it as a plain text file with the <c>.java</c> extension, compiling it to
      bytecode with the <c>javac</c> command, and then running it with the <c>java</c>
      command. Let’s go through that process in detail with an example.
    </p>

    <p>
      The tradition in programming, to test a new language or installation, is to write a
      program that prints Hello world to the screen. Let’s do that to learn how to compile
      and run programs at the command line.
    </p>

    <p>
      We will begin by writing a Java <q>Hello world</q> program in your text editor. To do
      this, open your favorite new text editor and type in <xref ref="listing-hello-world" />
      exactly as you see it here (being sure to match capitalization and punctuation) and
      then save it as plain text in your JavaPrograms directory with the name
      <c>Hello.java</c>.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-hello-world">
      <caption>Hello world program in <c>Hello.java</c>.</caption>
      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
<![CDATA[
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello world ");
    }
}
]]>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      Depending on the text editor you’re using, in Windows you can sometimes accidentally
      save a file with a double extension, such as <c>Hello.java.txt</c>. You will be able
      to see this double extension when you list the directory contents in the command line.
      If this happens, rename your file using the <c>ren</c> command. For example, <c>ren
      Hello.java.txt Hello.java</c> (and press Enter). If you need to rename a file in
      macOS or Linux, use the move command, for example, <c>mv Hello.java.txt
      Hello.java</c>. A Java program name will not contain any spaces, but if you are
      renaming another type of file, remember to use double quotes around your file name if
      it contains any spaces.
    </p>

    <p>
      What we have just created is a Java source file. A Java source file is a plain text
      file that contains Java code. The Java source file is named with the <c>.java</c>
      extension and must have the same name as the name following the words <c>public
      class</c> in the file.
    </p>

    <p>
      We want to run this program on the JVM we have just installed, but to do that,
      remember, we must first compile the program to Java bytecode. To do that, we use the
      <c>javac</c> command.
    </p>

    <p>
      Confirm that your working directory is the JavaPrograms directory and that you have
      saved the file in the JavaPrograms directory by navigating to JavaPrograms and typing
      either <c>dir</c> or <c>ls</c>, depending on what command-line program you’re using.
      You will see <c>Hello.java</c> in the directory. (If you don’t, solve that before you
      continue.) Now type <c>javac Hello.java</c> and press Enter.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you typed the program correctly and it is saved in your working directory, you
      will see the command prompt again after a brief pause. If you did not type the
      program correctly, then you will see some syntax errors, and if you’re in the wrong
      directory or the file isn’t there, you’ll see a file not found error. Correct any
      errors, type <c>javac Hello.java</c> again, and type <c>dir</c> or <c>ls</c>
      (depending on your command-line program), to see the new contents of the directory.
    </p>

    <p>
      You should now see a <c>Hello.class</c> file. This is your compiled <c>Hello.java</c>
      file – a file containing the Java bytecode that corresponds to the Hello world
      program. This is not a human-readable file. You can confirm that by opening it in your
      text editor. You’ll see some recognizable characters, but most of it will be
      unreadable to a text editor (and you). It is readable to the JVM, however! To run it,
      type <c>java Hello</c> at the command prompt. (Note that you do not include the
      <c>.class</c> extension when you run the <c>java</c> command.) You should see the text
      Hello world, followed by a new prompt. That is what the program does – it prints
      Hello world, and that is all, so the program finishes running and returns control to
      the command line.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once you have a <c>.class</c> file, you can run your program whenever you want. A
      couple of notes about this process: if you make changes to your <c>.java</c> file that
      result in syntax errors, a new <c>.class</c> file will not be created. If you then run
      your program with the <c>java</c> command and you still have an old <c>.class</c> file
      in the working directory, the old <c>.class</c> file will run. Similarly, if you edit
      your <c>.java</c> file but forget to save it, the <c>javac</c> command will compile the
      old, saved version of the file. Both of these situations can result in some confusion!
      The <c>javac</c> command will only create a <c>.class</c> file if you have a
      <c>.java</c> file with no syntax errors, and it will only work from a saved
      <c>.java</c> file in the working directory – not a version you have open and unsaved
      in your text editor.
    </p>

    <p>
      Starting in Java 11, you can also run a single-file program in one step by typing
      <c>java Hello.java</c>. The JVM compiles and executes the code entirely in memory
      without creating a <c>.class</c> file on disk. However, learning the two-step
      <c>javac</c> and <c>java</c> workflow is essential for understanding how Java
      bytecode works behind the scenes.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-1-5-3-eclipse">
    <title>Java in Eclipse</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-1-5-3-1-installing-eclipse">
      <title>Installing Eclipse</title>

      <p>
        While you can write Java programs using a basic text editor and the command line,
        professional developers typically use an Integrated Development Environment (IDE).
        An IDE combines (integrates) a code editor, compiler, debugger, and project manager
        into a single application. In this book, we use the free and open-source Eclipse
        IDE.
      </p>

      <p>
        To use Eclipse to develop Java programs, you’ll need to first download and install
        the Eclipse IDE for Java Developers. You’ll want to make sure it recognizes the JDK
        that you have already installed, and then you’ll be able to develop Java programs
        with it. We’ll go through the steps for downloading, installing, checking that it’s
        using the correct JDK, and writing Java programs here and in the next few sections.
      </p>

      <p>
        Eclipse provides an installer that you can use to install Eclipse. Follow the link
        on this page: <url
        href="https://www.eclipse.org/downloads/">https://www.eclipse.org/downloads/</url>
        and begin by downloading the correct installer for your operating system. (When you
        download, you will be prompted to make a donation. This is optional: you may use
        Eclipse for free.)
      </p>

      <p>
        When running the installer, choose <strong>Eclipse IDE for Java Developers</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        On the configuration screen that appears, confirm that Eclipse has selected your
        newly installed JDK in the Java VM field (if it hasn't, select your installed version
        from the dropdown menu, or click the Browse button to locate it). You can accept the
        default suggested Installation Folder. Ensure that the options to create desktop
        and start menu shortcuts are checked, then click Install.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-1-5-3-2-running-eclipse">
      <title>Running Eclipse</title>

      <p>
        Once installed, you should run Eclipse and test the installation. You can do this by
        clicking the installer’s launch button, or use one of the shortcuts.
      </p>

      <p>
        When Eclipse starts, it will ask you to select a Workspace. The Eclipse Workspace
        is a folder on your hard drive where Eclipse will store all of your Java projects,
        code, and preferences. You can accept the default folder or choose another folder.
        For now, you will probably only need one Workspace, but it is possible to have
        multiple Workspaces and to switch between them as needed.
      </p>

      <p>
        When a new Workspace is first opened, the Eclipse window will be filled by a large
        Welcome screen that includes links to extensive documentation and tutorials. You
        should close this screen, by clicking the x next to the word Welcome in the tab; you
        can get back to it later by choosing Help -> Welcome. (You might also see a request
        for a donation again; you can safely close that.)
      </p>

      <p>
        The Eclipse window is made up of several panes that contain one or more tabbed
        Views. A particular arrangement of these Views and editor panes is called a
        Perspective. You don’t need to worry about these names too much right now—they are
        mainly useful to know when you accidentally close something and need to find the
        menu command to get it back!
      </p>

      <p>
        When Eclipse opens (after you close the Welcome screen), your window is arranged in
        the default Java Perspective. By default, this layout consists of:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The Package Explorer (on the left): This view displays your files, organized
            neatly into folders and projects (we will create our first project in a
            moment).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The Editor Area (in the middle): This central area is where you will open, type,
            and edit your <c>.java</c> source code.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The Bottom Pane (utilities): This area holds several tabbed utility views, such
            as Problems and Javadoc. The Console view will also live here; although it may
            not be visible right now, Eclipse will automatically pop the Console open in
            this bottom pane the first time you run a program.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        You will also see an Outline view to the far right, which we can ignore for now.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is very common for beginners to accidentally close a tab or drag a pane into an
        awkward position. If you ever close an important view, you have two quick ways to fix
        it:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Restore an individual view: Go to Window-> Show View and select the name of the
            missing view (such as Package Explorer or Console).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Reset the entire window: To reset your workspace back to its original factory
            layout, choose Window->Perspective->Reset Perspective... and click Reset
            Perspective in the dialog box that pops up. Everything will immediately snap
            back to its proper place!
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        When you write Java code in Eclipse, you must put your code inside an Eclipse
        Project. To a software developer, a project is a container that groups together
        all the code, settings, and assets needed to build a complete program. While a
        professional software project might contain hundreds or thousands of individual
        Java files working together, your projects will be much smaller. We recommend
        creating a project to hold chapter source code and examples for each chapter as
        you’re learning, and a separate project to hold the code for each assignment you’re
        writing yourself. Therefore, you might have projects called Chapter1Programs,
        Chapter2Programs, Lab1, etc.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-1-5-3-3-hello-world-eclipse">
      <title>Writing Hello, world in Eclipse</title>

      <p>
        To create a Java project, go to choose File -> New -> Java Project. In the window
        that pops up, you will need to fill in a project name. If you decide to organize
        your programs by chapter, then Chapter1Programs is a reasonable name. You can
        organize your Java programs however you like, but as you’re just learning Eclipse,
        organizing by chapter and project is a good starting approach.
      </p>

      <p>
        Under the JRE section in the project dialog box, leave the Use an execution
        environment JRE option chosen, but make sure the number matches the JDK you
        installed. If that number is higher than the number you installed, choose the
        matching number from the drop-down.
      </p>

      <p>
        For projects you create while you’re learning, you should also uncheck the option
        labeled Create module-info.java file in the Module section. If you forget to do this,
        then delete the <c>module-info.java</c> file in your project, once created. If you
        don’t delete it, it will cause problems when you try to execute your programs.
      </p>

      <p>
        Once you have given your project a name, confirmed that the version of the JDK in the
        execution environment matches the version you installed, and unchecked the Create
        module-info.java box, you can click the Finish button.
      </p>

<figure xml:id="figure-eclipse-create-project">
        <caption>Creating a Java project in Eclipse</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_1_5.jpg" width="80%">
          <shortdescription>The Create New Project dialog box in Eclipse with recommended settings annotated.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              The Create New Project dialog box, annotated. The Project name is
              <c>Chapter1Programs</c> with the annotation <q>Create a project to hold the
              chapter 1 program examples.</q> The <q>use an execution environment JRE</q>
              radio button is selected, and in the drop-down, it says <q>JavaSE-25.</q> It is
              annotated <q>Use this option...</q>, and then below, the unselected option
              <q>Use default JRE</q> indicates <q>jdk-25.0.4</q> and is annotated <q>...but
              make sure the number matches the JDK you installed.</q> Under Module, the
              <q>Create module-info.java file</q> check box is unchecked, with the annotation
              <q>Uncheck this box.</q>
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The project should appear in the Package Explorer view on the left side of the
        Eclipse window. Click on the small triangle or plus sign next to the project name to
        see the contents of the project. Inside the project, you should see the JRE System
        Library (the collection of standard built-in classes that come with Java) and a
        folder called <c>src</c> (where your Java source code files will go). If you
        unchecked the module box: The <c>src</c> folder is currently empty, so it will not
        have an expand arrow next to it. This is the expected result. If you forgot to
        uncheck the module-info box: An expand arrow will appear next to <c>src</c>. If you
        see an arrow, click it to expand <c>src</c>, right-click on <c>module-info.java</c>,
        and choose Delete.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let’s now test your Eclipse installation by writing a <q>Hello world</q> program.
        Right-click on the <c>src</c> folder in the Package Explorer and choose New -> Class.
        In the dialog that opens, first check the Source folder. You should see your
        project name followed by <c>/src</c>, e.g. <c>Chapter1Programs/src</c>. If you do
        not see that, click the Browse button to the right and choose the <c>src</c> folder of
        your project.
      </p>

      <p>
        You should see nothing next to Package. If a Package is suggested, select the name
        and delete it.
      </p>

      <p>
        In the Name box, type <c>Hello</c>. We are going to write the same program we wrote
        and ran at the command line.
      </p>

      <p>
        The other default options should be fine. To save yourself some time, check the box
        next to <c>public static void main(String[] args)</c>, and click Finish.
      </p>

<figure xml:id="figure-eclipse-create-class">
        <caption>Creating a Java class in Eclipse</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_1_6.png" width="80%">
          <shortdescription>The New Java Class dialog box in Eclipse creating Hello.java.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              The New Java Class dialog box. Next to Source folder:
              <c>Chapter1Programs/src</c>. Next to Package: blank with <q>(default)</q>
              after. Enclosing type box unchecked. Name: <c>Hello</c>. All other boxes are
              defaults except the <c>public static void main(String[] args)</c> checkbox is
              checked.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        You will now see a window in the editing View with the title <c>Hello.java</c>. If
        you compare it to the Hello world program we wrote earlier (<xref
        ref="listing-hello-world" />), you can see that most of it is already filled in!
        There is a line that you should select, <c>// TODO Auto-generated method stub</c>,
        delete, and replace with the line that begins <c>System.out.println</c> in the program
        we wrote earlier. Once you have done that, save your edits and then choose Run ->
        Run As -> Java Application, or click the icon that looks like a play button in the
        toolbar above the editing window. If you typed the program correctly, it will run in
        the Console view below the editing window. In Eclipse, compiling and running are
        accomplished together with the Run command.
      </p>

      <p>
        Eclipse has several features that aid you as you type your code. A syntax error
        occurs if you put symbols together in a way that does not make sense to the
        compiler. If there are syntax errors in your program, Eclipse will underline the
        error with a squiggly red line, and will place a red x to the left of the line
        number. If you hover the mouse cursor over the x, a description of the error will
        appear. Some errors will pop up as you’re typing code and disappear once you
        complete that line of code, so you should ignore syntax errors until you have
        finished a line or block of code. For example, you may have noticed an error before
        you typed the semicolon at the end of the line that prints Hello world. Take a moment
        now to delete the semicolon and hover over the x and note the error. Try making
        other errors, such as deleting a quotation mark, parentheses, or curly brace, or
        changing the spelling or capitalization of words in the program and note the
        errors. Some are quite helpful, (e.g. <q>Syntax error, insert ‘)’ to complete
        expression</q>) and some probably won’t make sense until you’re a more experienced
        programmer (e.g. <q>system cannot be resolved</q>). That’s why it’s helpful to
        intentionally make mistakes and note the errors – so that when you unintentionally
        make them, you will remember the cause! (Be wary of Eclipse’s <q>quick fixes,</q>
        though. Do not choose one unless you understand the cause of the error and you know
        that the fix you’ve chosen is the correct fix. Otherwise the fix is likely to create
        more problems.)
      </p>

      <p>
        One of the most powerful features in Eclipse is Content Assist (often referred to
        as code completion or autocomplete). As you write Java code, you do not need to
        memorize every class name, method signature, or standard library package. By
        pressing <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Space</kbd> on Windows/Linux (or <kbd>Cmd</kbd> +
        <kbd>Space</kbd> on macOS), Eclipse inspects your current code context and displays
        a pop-up list of valid completions matching whatever you have typed so far.
        Selecting a suggestion from the list and pressing Enter inserts the remaining code
        automatically. This not only speeds up your typing, but it also eliminates spelling
        mistakes and case-sensitivity errors that would otherwise prevent your program from
        compiling.
      </p>

      <p>
        Content Assist is especially helpful when working with objects and classes. For
        example, typing a class or variable name followed immediately by a period (such as
        <c>System.</c> or <c>Math.</c>) prompts Eclipse to show every method available on
        that object, complete with its expected parameters, return type, and a preview of
        its official Javadoc documentation. Furthermore, Eclipse includes built-in code
        templates: typing common shortcuts such as <c>sysout</c> followed by
        <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Space</kbd> automatically expands into
        <c>System.out.println();</c>, and typing <c>main</c> expands into a complete
        <c>public static void main(String[] args)</c> method header. Using Content Assist
        regularly turns Eclipse from a simple text editor into an active partner that guides
        your learning.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-1-5-3-4-file-management-eclipse">
      <title>File Management with Eclipse</title>

      <p>
        When you create a project in Eclipse, a folder is created inside your Workspace
        folder. When you create a <c>.java</c> file, it is stored in the <c>src</c> folder
        inside the project folder in your Workspace folder. (Note that, although it is in the
        default package, there is no default package subfolder within <c>src</c>.) Take a
        moment to find the Workspace directory on your hard drive and compare the directory
        structure to what you see in Eclipse. If you don’t remember the directory that you
        chose for a Workspace, you can see the path by choosing File -> Switch Workspace, or
        you can right-click on a file in the Package Explorer and choose Show In -> System
        Explorer (or on macOS, Show In -> Finder). You now know where your source files are
        if you need to locate them. If you kept the default settings, when you compile your
        Java programs, the Java bytecode (<c>.class</c> files) will be stored in the
        <c>bin</c> folder.
      </p>

<figure xml:id="figure-package-explorer-system-explorer">
        <caption>The Package Explorer and System Explorer, compared</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_1_7.png" width="90%">
          <shortdescription>Comparison of Eclipse Package Explorer and the physical filesystem on disk.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              Package Explorer on the left and System Explorer on the right. On the left, the
              folder <c>Chapter1Programs</c>, and within it the JRE System Library and
              <c>src</c>. Within <c>src</c>, default package, and within default package,
              <c>Hello.java</c>. On the right, the physical <c>Chapter1Programs</c> folder
              contains the <c>.settings</c>, <c>bin</c>, and <c>src</c> folders, and the
              <c>.classpath</c> and <c>.project</c> files. An arrow points from <c>bin</c> to
              an open folder showing <c>Hello.class</c>. An arrow points from <c>src</c> to an
              open folder showing <c>Hello.java</c>.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        To run any of the sample Java programs from this textbook, you need to copy the
        source code file into your Eclipse Java project. First download the source code to
        your computer. You can then right-click on the default package in the project you
        want to import the code into and choose Import. Open General (by clicking the arrow
        next to it), choose File System, and click Next. You should then click the Browse
        button and use the file picker to select the folder that contains the source files.
        Click Select Folder. Once you do that, you’ll see all of the files in that folder
        with boxes next to them. Check the boxes of the <c>.java</c> source files that you
        want to import into your project. Confirm that they are going into the <c>src</c>
        folder of the correct project (and if not, use the Browse button to select the
        correct folder). Then click Finish. You will then see the file in the default folder
        of the <c>src</c> folder of your project. If a file gets imported into the wrong
        folder, you can simply drag it into the right place in the Package Explorer.
      </p>

      <p>
        Once a Java program is in the project, you can open it in an editor by
        double-clicking the file name in the Package Explorer.
      </p>

      <p>
        There are a couple of other ways you can import source files into an Eclipse project.
        You can drag the file icon from a System Explorer window onto the default folder in
        the Eclipse window. When you do, you’ll see a dialog box asking if you want to copy
        the file or link to the file. Choose to copy the file. You can also drag files
        directly between folders in the System Explorer, however, if you want to make a copy
        and not move the file, you should select and copy the file and then paste into the
        <c>src</c> folder of the Eclipse project. When you copy a file this way, you won’t
        immediately see a change in Eclipse. Once you’ve made a change in the System
        Explorer, right-click the project in Eclipse and choose Refresh to see the changes.
      </p>

      <p>
        One thing to note: if you copy a Java file into an Eclipse project, you will have
        two copies of the file. Changes that you make in Eclipse will be to the copy that’s
        in your Eclipse Workspace and not to the original file. This can be a good thing if
        you want to experiment without losing the original, working version of the file.
        However, it can be confusing to have multiple files with the same name.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-1-5-4-jshell">
    <title>Interactive Java with JShell</title>

    <p>
      Your JDK installation comes with a command-line environment: JShell. JShell allows you
      to type small pieces of Java code and see an immediate result. This is helpful for
      experimenting with Java features as you’re learning the language.
    </p>

    <p>
      To start JShell, run your command-line environment and type <c>jshell</c>, followed by
      Enter. You will get a JShell prompt where you can enter either a Java statement or a
      Java expression. If you enter a statement, it will be executed. If you enter an
      expression, its value will be printed. (You will learn the difference between
      statements and expressions in the next chapter.) In computer science, this kind of
      interactive tool is called a REPL (Read-Evaluate-Print Loop). It reads what you type,
      evaluates it, prints the result, and loops back to wait for your next command. Many
      languages, including Python and JavaScript, feature similar tools.
    </p>

    <p>
      Unlike standard Java programs which strictly require a semicolon (<c>;</c>) at the end
      of every statement, JShell allows you to omit the semicolon for convenience when
      typing single lines. Here is a short example of a JShell session, which you might type
      in for practice. You might try your own experiments based on the code shown here.
    </p>

    <console>
      <input>jshell</input>
      <output>
|  Welcome to JShell -- Version 25.0.4.1
|  For an introduction type: /help intro
      </output>
      <input>System.out.println("Hello World")</input>
      <output>
Hello World
      </output>
      <input>int x = 42</input>
      <output>
x ==&gt; 42
      </output>
      <input>x * x</input>
      <output>
$3 ==&gt; 1764
      </output>
      <input>/exit</input>
      <output>
|  Goodbye
      </output>
    </console>

    <p>
      Notice that when you evaluate an expression like <c>x * x</c>, JShell automatically
      creates a temporary variable (like <c>$3</c>) to store the result. You can actually use
      that variable name in subsequent calculations (for example, try typing <c>$3 + 10</c>).
    </p>

    <p>
      Using JShell can be a great way to experiment with Java as you’re learning. We will use
      it occasionally throughout the book to illustrate Java features.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>